#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125517} :activity/id #uuid "59fb40d7-580f-58af-a644-746cbf4df4bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050123 :topic/id #uuid "2eaffcb9-29e5-5903-a02d-080ab3e7a821" :topic/math-academy-id 1895} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125830 :tutorial/content "A **cylinder** is a surface made by the set of lines that pass through a plane curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane and are perpendicular to this plane. The curve $C$ is called the **base curve**.\n\nThe familiar two-dimensional conic sections (circles, ellipses, parabolas, and hyperbolas) can be used to describe cylinders. In this lesson, we'll explore these shapes.\n\nAn **elliptic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is an ellipse. If the ellipse lies in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of our elliptic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the semi-axes of the ellipse $C.$ In particular, if $a=b$, we get a **circular cylinder.**\n\n**Watch out!** This may look like the equation of a regular ellipse. However, since we're now describing a surface in three-dimensional space, we assume that the $z$-coordinate is arbitrary. Thus, if we want $z$ to feature in our equations explicitly, we would write\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1, \\qquad z=k$$ where $k\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nFor example, the graph of the elliptic cylinder\n\n$$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1$$\n\nis shown below. The center of its base ellipse $C$ is at the origin, and the **axis** of the elliptic cylinder (the line through the center of the cylinder) is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/50/504e609e134654d258378226d90f5faa8b9f6d12313a4fcac4a23d0bfe6bea52.png)\n\nLet's describe some properties of this elliptic cylinder:\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1,$$ which is the base ellipse $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0)$ with semi-major and semi-minor axes of lengths $2$ and $1,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/d0/d0e4c0630b0da60c4324330211ab1bf8090ebec067d9ebc2ac2a03056c500bd6.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/38/3813a5f43319056be03ddd8b3353ef3a8166b7495b32397c91066f40d0b33cd5.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two lines in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d928060ad18d3ba677f5dd184777b833d3abf03226c04c21292e455bcd0518.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f8f004e2-d43f-5628-bbbe-4b3c7918a9f5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4931 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "21ce271d-74a1-526d-a3bb-1e1dc0da83ed" :step/math-academy-id 30776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124587 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124398 :question/id #uuid "0cb849bd-8a9f-5305-b086-62da1a10e54f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8226"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401495}] :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"} :step/id #uuid "6b214232-840a-58b5-8946-bbbc69c878d7" :step/math-academy-id 30778 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125831 :tutorial/content "A **parabolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a parabola.\n\nIf the parabola $C$ opens upward or downward when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by $$(x-x_0)^2 = 4p(y-y_0),$$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder $$x^2 = y$$ is shown below. Its base curve is an upward-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/03/03ab0a28802900d8076fdd5f555bdad6582c449441c46f1de74260013cc55119.png)\n\nThe trace of this parabolic cylinder can be either a parabola or a set of lines.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$x^2 = y,$$ which corresponds to the base parabola $C$ with its vertex at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/98/98124890d921c73d0cda54428ac803221d5f393384e14db2909b0138fda0fb93.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a line in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2} & =y \\\\ y & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a line in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\nOn the other hand, if the base parabola $C$ is left-opening or right-opening when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by $$(y-y_0)^2 = 4p(x-x_0),$$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder $$y^2 = x$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a right-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/a8/a87267dbd77b1561c047c6228ba0c70b08e5a48228d7e0b6a161463045d5e22b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4026d216-b14f-558d-a7a4-cf39aa550b45" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4932 :tutorial/title "Parabolic Cylinders"} :step/id #uuid "0e4c9032-faa3-5e93-a91c-a0a7db82838a" :step/math-academy-id 30779 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124588 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124399 :question/id #uuid "64fc4653-a2ba-5013-8c70-1f4f14e5f8be" :question/math-academy-id "e-8227"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401474}] :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"} :step/id #uuid "595c41f8-1c2f-5e89-b663-e36a44f691ac" :step/math-academy-id 30781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125834 :tutorial/content "A **hyperbolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a hyperbola.\n\nIf the hyperbola $C$ is horizontal in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by $$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,$$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder $$x^2 - y^2 = 1$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a horizontal hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e3/e3c921cc7d6a1edbb182ecf158d7b84eb0d203fd20924d07cf57ca8f8ffdc107.png)\n\nThe trace of this surface can be either a hyperbola, a set of lines, or the empty set.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$x^2 - y^2 = 1,$$ which is the base hyperbola $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/19/19e02f27f625dd75d12333de3b82a7a012e4f6370c729eef5cd38a633c2c8689.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a pair of lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/56/5679b53433493d8b6e92d5aa5273be30bbf67a9db51993a925423d8514571f05.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is the empty set.\n\nOn the other hand, if the base hyperbola $C$ is vertical when viewed from the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by $$\\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} = 1,$$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder $$y^2 - x^2 = 1$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a vertical hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin, and the cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/d6/d690bca9515389bb403d0f205861af3294bc3a961df6691a5adb86de01421cff.png)\n\nNote that for all of the cylinders discussed so far, the *domain* of each cylinder in the $xy$-plane is simply the base curve $C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7260540c-2b7c-555f-9aa0-6f3dd490079b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4939 :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Cylinders"} :step/id #uuid "3f461a0c-aa8c-58dd-b9c4-e57ae5d51a3b" :step/math-academy-id 30810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125884 :tutorial/content "So far, we've encountered cylinders whose axes are parallel to the $z$-axis. However, we can also define cylinders whose axes are parallel to the $x$- or $y$-axes. To do this, we simply need to describe a base curve in either the $yz$-plane or the $xz$-plane. \n\nFor an elliptic cylinder whose base ellipse $C$ is centered at the origin, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- Consider the elliptic cylinder $C$ whose base curve lies in the $xz$-plane.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1\n$$\n\nIn this case, the axis of the elliptic cylinder is the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f3/f32db327a6e1f54d5b346af4ea2f9b0bcfce7f82c1b6e44375f398705acd4f63.png)\n\n- Consider the elliptic cylinder $C$ whose base curve lies in the $yz$-plane.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1\n$$\n\nIn this case, the axis of the elliptic cylinder is the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/53/5395c07f7646db4d4091e4bf176ae8d13c9c6753c322b8325dee2542480efdd4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "12af86a6-3212-596e-a78e-92fb9ba06344" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7851 :tutorial/title "Elliptic Cylinders With Alternative Central Axes"} :step/id #uuid "2800e74a-ab30-5499-809e-31fb6deb9785" :step/math-academy-id 45321 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125886 :tutorial/content "For a parabolic cylinder whose base parabola $C$ has its vertex at the origin, the other four cases are as follows:\n\n- When $C$ is in the $xz$-plane and $p>0,$ the axis is the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/79/79d1fdd86e7f615597125f5ac43e55ecd59bc298387575707348784d2d48acd0.png)\n\nNote that if $p < 0,$ we get a reflection in the $xy$-plane or $yz$-plane of the parabolic cylinders shown above.\n\n- When $C$ is in the $yz$-plane and $p>0,$ the axis is the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/73/730dccdd02e06d5f2de834f7b42c9121246aacda7595d2c5f760ae2f4c3bb5c9.png)\n\nNote that if $p<0,$ we get a reflection in the $xy$-plane or $xz$-plane of the parabolic cylinders shown above." :tutorial/id #uuid "049a63bf-b3e2-52e8-80c1-82042790f8f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7855 :tutorial/title "Parabolic Cylinders With Alternative Central Axes"} :step/id #uuid "8fa4a35a-4cb0-5fd5-99f4-903b12b2986b" :step/math-academy-id 45325 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125887 :tutorial/content "For a hyperbolic cylinder whose base hyperbola $C$ has its vertex at the origin, the other four cases are as follows:\n\n- When $C$ is in the $xz$-plane, the axis of the hyperbolic cylinder is the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/28/28f79c28aa6dfaa28b7b8ddd6d196bcb21db18411454c655a3365be52e8c6bc7.png)\n\n- When $C$ is in the $yz$-plane, the axis of the hyperbolic cylinder is the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/57/5763cb9b167c2e1e9d17c9778bf569a0efafb4fe36f97e05e8e2173fb29fc998.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "dba66b44-ad67-5d83-b191-e1d2f8b59ff8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7856 :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Cylinders With Alternative Central Axes"} :step/id #uuid "85399770-a72b-521d-b90e-0e8d27d7e807" :step/math-academy-id 45326 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125520}}] :activity/title "Cylinders" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124698}]}
